{"id":"a4f2e004749ee21ee7361f46010ffa7affde398d","axis":"silent","repo":"elsa-workflows/elsa-core","sha":"a4f2e004749ee21ee7361f46010ffa7affde398d","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"core/docs/migrations/tenant-scoped-definition-ids.md","lines":["@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@","+# Workflow definition ids are unique per tenant","+","+**Schema change.** With a shared database, the same workflow definition can now exist in more than one tenant. The unique index on `WorkflowDefinitions` includes `TenantId`.","+","+## EF Core","+","+- `IX_WorkflowDefinition_DefinitionId_Version` on `(DefinitionId, Version)` is replaced by `IX_WorkflowDefinition_DefinitionId_TenantId_Version` on `(DefinitionId, TenantId, Version)`, still unique.","+- The new `TenantScopedDefinitionIds` migration in the Management context drops and recreates the index for SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle and SQLite. Existing rows satisfy the wider key, so `Up()` cannot fail on data.","+- The default tenant is `\"\"`, not `NULL`, like the label uniqueness migration (`PerTenantLabelUniqueness`). PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite treat `NULL`s as distinct in a unique index, so `Up()` stamps leftover `NULL` `TenantId` values to `\"\"` there (and on SQL Server), and `EFCoreWorkflowDefinitionStore` now stores `\"\"` instead of `NULL` when multitenancy is off. Without this, two concurrent imports of the same new definition could both create version 1.","+- Oracle stores `''` as `NULL`, but treats rows with equal non-null columns and a `NULL` `TenantId` as duplicates in a unique index, so the index stays unique for the default tenant without stamping. SQL Server also treats `NULL`s as equal. The index has no filter.","+- On SQL Server the key is `nvarchar(450)` + `nvarchar(450)` + `int`, up to 1804 bytes, above the 1700-byte limit for a nonclustered index key. The migration succeeds with a warning, and an insert fails only when `DefinitionId` and `TenantId` together exceed about 848 characters.","+- `Down()` restores the old index. It fails while two tenants hold the same `(DefinitionId, Version)`; remove or rename one of them first. On Oracle and MySQL, where DDL commits implicitly, `Down()` checks for such duplicates before dropping the new index, so a failed downgrade leaves the unique index in place.","+","+## Import","+","+- `WorkflowDefinitionImporter` keeps the exported version id (`model.Id`, the primary key) only when no tenant uses it. Otherwise the new definition gets a generated version id and keeps its definition id.","+- Re-importing into the tenant that already has the definition updates the existing draft and is unchanged.","+- `DefaultWorkflowDefinitionStorePopulator` follows the same rule for the rows it creates: a tenant-specific provider (blob, file) that serves the same workflow to several tenants of a shared store keeps its version id in the first tenant and gets a generated one in the others. Existing rows (same tenant, same definition id and version) are updated in place, and the version ids of tenant-agnostic (`*`) workflows are never replaced. The \"already exists in another tenant\" guard of the Dapper store stays for genuine primary key conflicts. From the first startup, for each such row in every tenant but the first (twice per startup, because the registries are populated in two passes), the populator logs at debug that the stored id differs from the provider's id, since that is the intended state. It logs a warning only when no other tenant uses the provider's id.","+- A provider workflow with a blank `Identity.Id` in a non-`*` tenant now gets a generated version id. Callers that registered it through `IWorkflowRegistry.RegisterAsync` and dispatch by a handle with an empty version id must use the definition id instead.","+- A child workflow pinned in a parent by `WorkflowDefinitionVersionId` and `Version` is looked up in this order: the version id, then the pinned `Version` number of the same definition in the current tenant unless that version is an unpublished latest draft, then the published version, then the latest. The fallback after the version id applies to every version-id miss, including a single-tenant import from another environment, where an import into an existing definition creates the next version with a generated id. In a second tenant the pinned id belongs to the first tenant's row. A new import always starts at version 1, so the pinned number only lines up when the child was exported at version 1; for any other pinned version the parent runs the published (or latest) version of the child.","+","+## What to do","+","+- A custom construction of `WorkflowDefinitionImporter` that uses the old constructor still compiles but is obsolete and keeps the exported version id even when another tenant uses it; use the constructor that accepts an `IWorkflowDefinitionStore`.","+- MongoDB needs no manual step. At startup it creates the unique index `DefinitionId_1_TenantId_1_Version_1` on `(DefinitionId, TenantId, Version)` first, then drops the legacy store-wide `DefinitionId_1_Version_1`. Default-tenant rows have a null `TenantId`, so they stay unique per `(DefinitionId, Version)`.","+- A MongoDB warning is logged, and the index is kept, when a store-wide unique `(DefinitionId, Version)` index under another name exists (tenants still cannot share a definition until you drop it), or when a non-unique `(DefinitionId, TenantId, Version)` index blocks the new one while the legacy index remains (drop the non-unique one and restart). If that non-unique index exists and nothing else keeps definition versions unique, startup fails with the same instruction.","+- Known limitation: a tenant should not reuse the definition id of a tenant-agnostic (`*`) CLR workflow. The unique index treats `*` as its own tenant, so a tenant that saves the same `(DefinitionId, Version)` ends up with two rows that both resolve for that tenant.","+- Dapper has no unique index on `(DefinitionId, Version)`, so no schema change is needed. `DapperWorkflowDefinitionStore.AnyAsync` now honors `WorkflowDefinitionFilter.TenantAgnostic`, which the importer and the populator rely on to see version ids used by other tenants."],"additions":28,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"core/src/modules/Elsa.Persistence.EFCore.MySql/Migrations/Management/20261101100003_TenantScopedDefinitionIds.Designer.cs","lines":["@@ -0,0 +1,249 @@","+﻿// <auto-generated />","+using System;","+using Elsa.Persistence.EFCore.Modules.Management;","+using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;","+using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Infrastructure;","+using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Metadata;","+using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Migrations;","+using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Storage.ValueConversion;","+","+#nullable disable","+","+namespace Elsa.Persistence.EFCore.MySql.Migrations.Management","+{","+    [DbContext(typeof(ManagementElsaDbContext))]","+    [Migration(\"20261101100003_TenantScopedDefinitionIds\")]","+    partial class TenantScopedDefinitionIds","+    {","+        /// <inheritdoc />","+        protected override void BuildTargetModel(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)","+        {","+#pragma warning disable 612, 618","+            modelBuilder","+                .HasDefaultSchema(\"Elsa\")","+                .HasAnnotation(\"ProductVersion\", \"9.0.17\")","+                .HasAnnotation(\"Relational:MaxIdentifierLength\", 64);","+","+            MySqlModelBuilderExtensions.AutoIncrementColumns(modelBuilder);","+","+            modelBuilder.Entity(\"Elsa.Workflows.Management.Entities.WorkflowDefinition\", b =>","+                {","+                    b.Property<string>(\"Id\")","+                        .HasColumnType(\"varchar(255)\");","+","+                    b.Property<byte[]>(\"BinaryData\")","+                        .HasColumnType(\"longblob\");","+","+                    b.Property<DateTimeOffset>(\"CreatedAt\")","+                        .HasColumnType(\"datetime(6)\");","+","+                    b.Property<string>(\"Data\")"